===== docs/plans/261007-csat-rating/decisions.md =====
# Decisions and recommended answers

You were away, so each question below carries the answer I recommend and the plan is written on it. Change any
answer and tell me; the phase that depends on it is named.

| # | Question | Recommended answer | Cost of the other choice | Phase |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| D1 | Requirement 6 says rename `assignee_id` "everywhere". The nightly export (`src/jobs/export-tickets.js:9-13`) writes tickets as stored and finance's job reads it. What does the export file call the field? | Rename in the code, and write both `owner_id` and `assignee_id` (same value) in `exports/tickets.json`. Drop `assignee_id` from the file in a later change, after finance confirms. | Renaming in the file too breaks finance's report the night it ships, and their deploy is not ours to time. Keeping only the old name leaves "everywhere" unmet. | 1 |
| D2 | Production is Postgres behind the same store interface, not in this repo. Who changes the database and the nightly runner? | Not this plan. Phases 1 and 2 write the SQL and the runner entry as hand-over notes (expand, backfill, then drop the old column later). Whoever owns the Postgres adapter and the job schedule applies them, in that order, before the code ships. | Doing it here means writing an adapter I cannot see or test. | 1, 2, 5 |
| D3 | Can a closed ticket be rated? Spec: "resolved". Glossary: closed is never changed again. | No. Only `resolved`. | Allowing it means rating writes to a closed ticket, against the glossary, and the purge job (`src/jobs/purge-closed.js`) leaves the row, so it is possible but is a policy change. The cost of "no": a customer who rates after day three is refused. | 2 |
| D4 | Where does the rating live? | On the ticket row: `rating_score` and `rated_at`, both `null` until rated. The export therefore gains two fields (additive). | A separate table needs a join for every read and a second write path. | 2 |
| D5 | Who may rate, and how is a refusal shown? | Any customer of the ticket's organisation, not only the one who opened it. A missing ticket and another organisation's ticket both return `null`, as `getMyTicket` does (`src/portal.js:12`). Every other refusal (bad score, wrong status, already rated) throws an `Error` with a plain message, as `reply` does. The organisation check runs before the score check so a bad score cannot reveal that a foreign ticket exists. | Telling a foreign ticket apart from a missing one leaks that the id exists. | 2 |
| D6 | What counts as a whole number from 1 to 5? | `Number.isInteger(score)` and 1 to 5. The string `"4"`, `4.5`, `NaN`, `null` and `undefined` are refused; the portal layer converts form input before calling. | Accepting `"4"` hides a caller bug. | 2 |
| D7 | "At most one rating" under two simultaneous submissions. | The store gets `rateTicket(id, { score, at })`, which writes only when no rating exists and returns `null` otherwise. In Postgres this is `UPDATE ... WHERE id = $1 AND rating_score IS NULL RETURNING *`. A read-then-write in the portal would let two submissions both succeed. | Check-then-write works in the in-memory store and fails in production. | 2 |
| D8 | A score of 1 or 2 on a ticket with no owner. `notifyAgent` throws without an agent (`src/notify.js:7`). | Store the rating, queue no email, do not fail the call. | Failing after the write would leave a rating stored with an error shown to the customer. An alert to a team-lead queue is not in the spec. | 3 |
| D9 | What is the weekly summary and where is it written? | A row per agent per night, saved in the store: `{ agent_id, period_end, window_days: 7, ratings, average }`, one row per agent per day (a second run the same day replaces it). The window is ratings with `rated_at` at most seven days before the run (seven days exactly counts, eight does not). Attributed to the ticket's current owner. Average rounded to two decimals. Agents with no ratings in the window get no row. | Writing a file under `exports/` would put it next to finance's input; it is not for them. | 4 |
| D10 | What does the low-score email contain? | Event `{ type: 'low_rating', ticketId, score }` through the existing `notifyAgent`, queued after the rating is stored. No customer text exists to include. | None of note. | 3 |
| D11 | A rated ticket is reopened, or the customer replies, then it is resolved again. | The rating stays and the ticket still cannot be rated a second time ("at most one rating"). Status changes never clear it. | Clearing it lets one customer rate repeatedly and re-fire the email. | 2 |

## Not decided here, for you

- Whether the finance team has a date for switching to `owner_id` (D1). Until then the alias stays.
- Whether anything outside this repo (the back-office UI, other reports) reads `assignee_id` (D1, D2).

